clustervision — search, read, and port AI agent sessions across harnesses

Usage: cv <COMMAND>

Options:
  -h, --help     Print help
  -V, --version  Print version


Commands:
  Read
    ls         List discovered sessions across all harnesses
    show       Print a single session (by `harness:id` or a unique id prefix)
    cat        Print one tool call's full output, wherever it lives: inline in the transcript, in a `prune` sidecar, or in a persisted-output file. `--input` prints the call's arguments instead
    search     Full-text search across all session content
    events     List what a session DID: its extracted events (file edits/reads, commands, errors)
    touched    Sessions that touched a file — every session with a file_edit/file_read event on its path
    tools      Cross-agent tool analytics: per-agent histograms, which-agent-used-what, aggregate usage, and a tool-call timeline — across the orchestrator and its whole sub-agent forest
    workflow   A `Workflow`-tool run, first-class: its phase tree, the agents under each phase with their outcomes, run totals, and the driving script. Without `<run_id>`, lists the session's runs
    lanes      The sub-agent forest as a status table: one row per lane with its model, start, duration, tokens, tool calls, status and last line — and the strand class (`--stranded`): lanes that stopped saying they are waiting for something that will never wake them. `--recent N` reads the forests of a project's N most recent sessions instead of one session's; with `--unfinished` that is the rescue list for `cv adopt`
    deferrals  A linter for promises: every place the assistant deferred something ("later lane", "follow-up", "not tonight", "queued", "your call", "when X lands", …) with its message index and context. The phrase table is `--phrases-file`, else `<config>/deferrals.txt` if present, else a built-in default. `--open-tasks` cross-references the task store and marks each MATCHED or UNMATCHED; any UNMATCHED exits 1, so a closeout can gate on it
    stats      Fleet analytics over all discovered sessions
    blame      Code provenance: which agent session wrote this code, and what was it thinking?
    doctor     Diagnose why a session's context window keeps filling and compacting: attribute context pressure by source (tool results split MCP/builtin, thinking, messages), size the fixed system+tools overhead from token usage, and pair it with compaction frequency. With no <id>, analyzes the most recent session(s) for the current directory
  Reshape
    prune      Custom, lossless compaction of a Claude session into a NEW resumable session: snip bulky old tool payloads (large reads/logs/screenshots) into a sidecar, leaving a small `[PRUNED]` marker — your prompts and flow stay verbatim, the recent turns stay sharp. Resume with `claude --resume <new-id>`; fetch a stashed original back with `cv cat <new-id> <tool_use_id>`
    rewind     Rewind a Claude session to a past moment: a NEW resumable session holding the source's records up to a message or the tool result that made a commit — the agent as it was when it landed that code, ready for `claude --resume <new-id> --fork-session`. Starts at the last compaction before the cut (the context the agent actually had); a sub-agent transcript comes out as a standalone top-level session. Writes `<new-id>.rewind.json` provenance alongside
    distill    Distill a long session (typically a sub-agent lane) into a compact context: the brief and every message to the agent verbatim, its own words (text, messages sent, notes written) verbatim, an index of commits/branches/hosts/paths/verdicts/errors, a one-line-per-call ledger, and its last N tool calls as real turns. Deterministic (no model calls). Elided outputs go to a sidecar `cv cat` reads. Prints the markdown pack by default; `--session` emits a resumable session (`claude -p --resume <id>`); `--agent-of <root>` emits a new sub-agent of that root, which the root resumes with SendMessage to the printed agent id
    adopt      Rescue sub-agents stranded by a dead Claude Code session into a live one, so the live session can `SendMessage` them and they resume with their whole transcript: copies `subagents/agent-<id>.jsonl` (with `sessionId` restamped on every line, nothing else changed) and its `.meta.json` into the live session's `subagents/`. Find the agents with `cv lanes <session>` (one session) or `cv lanes --recent N --unfinished` (the project's recent sessions)
    splice     Compose a new session from spans of existing ones (`<id>:A..B`): a verbatim prefix (`<id>:..N`) forks a lane at message N; two spans graft one session's continuation onto another's head. `--session` emits a resumable Claude session, `--agent-of <root>` a new sub-agent of that root (as `distill` does); `--harness`/`--out` emit to any harness; with neither, a summary prints (`--export md|json` prints the composed session)
    port       Produce a copy of a session that runs elsewhere: in another harness (`--harness`), from another working directory (`--cwd`, carrying CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md/… along), or both. The source is never touched; the source harness rides on the id (`codex:019e…`)
    resume     Print (or with --launch, run) the resume incantation for a session in its native harness
  Export
    export     Export a session to markdown or OpenSession JSON (stdout); `cv share` is the HTML artifact
    dataset    Export the corpus as a fine-tuning dataset (JSONL, one session per line). `chatml`/`sharegpt` import directly into Unsloth Studio / TRL / HF — no adapter
    pack       Compile a context bundle for a new task from your whole session corpus
  Fleet & live
    task       Fleet task substrate: dispatch, review, and git-observed landing
    scry       Follow live agent activity across harnesses (tail -f for sessions)
    watch      Follow live activity and archive every session as it changes, forever (archives what already exists on the first poll). `cv scry` is the same feed without the archive
    archive    The durable archive: every session snapshotted as IR under one home, for search and safekeeping across machines (`sync` once, `ls`, `path`; `cv watch` keeps it current)
    serve      One local HTTP server: the read API at /api/* (what the dashboard and app read), the task inbox at /inbox (decisions as buttons; writes record the same events the CLI does, as `web:<who>`), and with `--web DIR` the dashboard at /. Loopback only unless `--host` is non-loopback AND a token (or `--insecure-expose`) is given
    mcp        The MCP server (stdio JSON-RPC): every Read/Reshape/Export/System command as a tool, built from this binary's own clap tree, plus `await_omen`, `observe_stream` and the task tools. Register it with `claude mcp add clustervision -- /abs/path/to/cv mcp`
    share      Redact a session and emit a single self-contained HTML artifact anyone can open
  System
    index      Build/refresh the tantivy full-text index that makes `cv search` instant
    config     View the user config (`~/.config/clustervision/config.toml`) and manage the export-source index. With no flag, prints the config path + registered export sources. Account data exports (ChatGPT/Claude.ai `conversations.json`) have no fixed home, so register the dirs/files you want the `chatgpt-export`/`claude-export` harnesses to discover
    schema     The reference: the `-q` query calculus (every field, operator, example) and, with `--json`, the machine-readable schema of every shape cv emits (session row, message, block, query). `--commands --json` dumps the whole command tree for tool generation
    formats    The format census: what the most recent sessions actually contain, per harness — message kinds, block types and the record vocabulary the adapter did not interpret. Types the manifest does not name are marked `NEW`, the drift signal
    recipes    The agent quickstart: the things agents do with cv, each as one command line with the JSON keys it returns
    hooks      Wire cv into Claude Code: install / remove / show the five hooks (`cv hook …`) and the `cv` skill in its settings. Idempotent and reversible; agents set this up for themselves
    hook       One hook's body, run by Claude Code: reads the hook JSON on stdin, prints a line or two of hook JSON (or nothing). `cv hooks install claude` writes the entries that call these

